## Phase 2 — The Memory: Library Analyst (Weeks 2–3)

The piece nobody ships. An MCP server that knows every sound you own.

### 2.1 Scanner (`scanner.py`)

- [ ] Walk your sample directories (start with one folder to test, then the full library)
- [ ] For each audio file (`.wav`, `.aif`, `.mp3`, `.flac`): store path, filename, size, hash → *a fingerprint of the file's contents, so re-scans skip unchanged files*
- [ ] Store in SQLite → *a database that's just a single file — no server to run, perfect here*

### 2.2 Analysis (`analysis.py`)

Per file, extract with **librosa** (`pip install librosa`):

| Feature | What it tells us | Plain English |
|---------|-----------------|---------------|
| BPM | `librosa.beat.beat_track` | tempo of loops |
| Key/pitch | chroma features | what note/key a sample sits in |
| Duration | — | one-shot vs. loop |
| Spectral centroid | brightness score | dark rumble vs. bright sparkle |
| Onset strength | punchiness | soft pad vs. hard transient |
| RMS loudness | energy | quiet texture vs. full-power stab |

- [ ] Optional power-up: **CLAP embeddings** → *a model that turns audio into coordinates in "meaning space," so the text "dark gritty texture" can literally be matched against sounds by distance.* (`pip install laion-clap`) Adds true vibe search.

### 2.3 Database schema

```sql
CREATE TABLE samples (
  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,
  path TEXT UNIQUE, hash TEXT,
  bpm REAL, key TEXT, duration REAL,
  brightness REAL, punch REAL, loudness REAL,
  category TEXT,        -- kick/snare/bass/texture/vocal/fx (folder-name heuristics first)
  embedding BLOB        -- CLAP vector, if enabled
);
```

### 2.4 MCP server (`mcp_server.py`)

Expose these tools (use the official `mcp` Python SDK):

- [ ] `find_sounds(description, key=None, bpm_range=None, category=None, limit=10)` — the star
- [ ] `similar_to(path)` — "more like this one"
- [ ] `analyze_file(path)` — full readout of any single file
- [ ] `library_stats()` — coverage report: what you have lots of, what you lack
- [ ] Register as a second server in Claude Desktop config alongside XLNT-Ableton
- [ ] **The magic test:** "Find me a dark textured bass one-shot near F minor and load it onto a new audio track." (analyst finds → Ableton server loads — two servers cooperating)

### 2.5 Build order with Claude Code

1. [ ] Scanner on ONE folder → verify database rows appear
2. [ ] Analysis on those files → sanity-check BPM/key against files you know
3. [ ] Full library scan (run overnight; 80K files takes hours)
4. [ ] MCP wrapper → wire into Claude Desktop
5. [ ] CLAP embeddings last (it's the cherry, not the cake)

